CPython 3.14.7 bundles HACL*'s lowstar_endianness.h, whose generic
big-endian fallback defines htole64 with htobe32 on each half. On a
big-endian machine htobe32 is the identity, so that macro exchanges the
two 32-bit halves and reverses nothing inside them: every 64-bit
little-endian store is wrong.

MD5 is what notices. Its padding ends with the message length as a
little-endian 64-bit integer, so the digest of the empty string is
correct -- zero survives any permutation -- and the digest of anything
else is not:

    md5(b"abc") = 4e949884141cdf648a8f92f5502abd2f
           want   900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72

SHA-1 and SHA-2 are unaffected: they are big-endian formats.

The fallback is only reached on a big-endian platform that does not
have <endian.h> (the first branch of the file), which is why this has
gone unnoticed -- big-endian Linux takes the first branch.

diff -ru a/Modules/_hacl/include/krml/lowstar_endianness.h b/Modules/_hacl/include/krml/lowstar_endianness.h
--- a/Modules/_hacl/include/krml/lowstar_endianness.h	2026-09-22 16:41:05.646431936 -0600
+++ b/Modules/_hacl/include/krml/lowstar_endianness.h	2026-09-22 16:41:05.659951101 -0600
@@ -115,11 +115,26 @@
 
 #  define htobe64(x) (x)
 #  define be64toh(x) (x)
+/*
+ * htole64 on a big-endian machine must reverse ALL EIGHT BYTES. This
+ * used htobe32 on each half, which on a big-endian machine is the
+ * identity -- so it exchanged the two halves and reversed nothing
+ * inside them, and every 64-bit little-endian store came out wrong.
+ *
+ * What it breaks is MD5, whose padding ends with the message length as
+ * a little-endian 64-bit integer. The digest of the empty string is
+ * right, because its length is zero and zero survives any permutation;
+ * every other digest is wrong. SHA-1 and SHA-2 are unaffected, being
+ * big-endian formats.
+ *
+ * htole32 below is the byte-reversing one, which is what each half
+ * needs.
+ */
 #  define htole64(x)                                                           \
     (__extension__({                                                           \
       uint64_t __temp = (x);                                                   \
-      uint32_t __low = htobe32((uint32_t)__temp);                              \
-      uint32_t __high = htobe32((uint32_t)(__temp >> 32));                     \
+      uint32_t __low = htole32((uint32_t)__temp);                              \
+      uint32_t __high = htole32((uint32_t)(__temp >> 32));                     \
       (((uint64_t)__low) << 32) | __high;                                      \
     }))
 #  define le64toh(x) (htole64((x)))
